# Access rules for the installer directory.
#
# install/step4.php imports install/phpnuxbill.sql, which opens with
# DROP TABLE IF EXISTS for all 21 tables and has no install lock of its own.
# Anyone who can reach it can wipe the database, after which step5.php hands
# them a fresh administrator account.
#
# THE LOCK ITSELF IS NOT HERE. It is install/guard.php, which refuses to run
# whenever install/.installed exists. step5.php writes that marker as its final
# action, so the installer locks itself with no manual step. guard.php is plain
# PHP, so unlike this file it cannot be ignored by the server config and cannot
# mis-parse. Do not try to move the lock back into this file.
#
# This file therefore only hides the handful of files that must never be
# fetched over HTTP, and it does so with the oldest, most widely supported
# directives available: <Files> with an exact name, one per file, no regex, no
# nested or negated <IfModule>, no <IfFile>.
#
# That restraint is deliberate and was learned the hard way. An earlier version
# of this file used <FilesMatch> plus <IfFile> plus nested
# <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c> fallbacks. On LiteSpeed it was misparsed into a
# blanket deny: the installer's own stylesheet, logo and every step after the
# first all returned 403, so the wizard was unusable and unstyled, while
# index.php still loaded. On Apache 2.4 the <IfFile> block was separately
# measured to be a silent no-op. Neither construct earned its complexity.
#
# The .sql files are read straight off disk by step4.php via file_get_contents(),
# which this file does not affect, so denying them here costs the installer
# nothing. Without it, a deployed server publishes its full database schema at a
# fixed and well-known path.
#
# Apache 2.4 syntax, which is what cPanel/LiteSpeed implements.

<Files "phpnuxbill.sql">
    Require all denied
</Files>

<Files "radius.sql">
    Require all denied
</Files>

<Files "guard.php">
    Require all denied
</Files>

<Files "update.php">
    Require all denied
</Files>
